Setting, taken from the neighbouring pages. L is a totally ordered set (a 'line'). A drapeau (flag) is a finite chain T = {t1<...<tn} in L. Its generalized strata are the points t_i together with the interstitial intervals [I_{<t1}], I_{t1,t2}, ..., I_{t_{n-1},t_n}, [I_{>tn}]. The end intervals appear only if t1 is not minimal, resp. tn not maximal, in L. The poset Û (written 𝔘̂) is the set of all such strata. The relation t ◁ X means the point t is an endpoint of the interval X. A Û-polyfigure Φ is a finite set of strata. δΦ is the flag of all points occurring in Φ or as endpoints of intervals of Φ. Φ̂ is the largest polyfigure with the same flag, i.e. all strata of δΦ. A polyfigure Φ amounts to the flag T plus a set of strata with the covering condition: for every t ∈ T, either t ∈ Φ or t ◁ X for some interval X ∈ Φ.

This page defines the complementary polyfigure Φ* = Φ̂ \ Φ: the strata of δΦ that are not in Φ.

Claim 1: δΦ* = δΦ \ Φ_red =: T'. Here Φ_red = {s ∈ Φ_0 : ord(s,Φ) = 2} is the set of redundant vertices, i.e. points of Φ both of whose adjacent intervals lie in Φ.

This leads to a digression on the five vertex types of a μ̂-polygon, with a cross-reference to pp. 110-111:
- isolated vertices;
- proper boundary vertices;
- improper boundary vertices;
- proper interior vertices (non-redundant);
- improper interior vertices (redundant).

Claim 2: Φ* has no redundant vertices, so δΦ** = δΦ*. Proof: a vertex of Φ* is some t ∉ Φ. By the covering condition, some interval adjacent to t lies in Φ, so ord(t,Φ*) ≤ 1.

Claim 3: Φ** is the set of strata X of T' for which some Y ∈ Φ satisfies Y ≪ X. The next page names this 'effacement des éléments redondants', realized by composing the intervals adjacent to redundant vertices. There:
- (Φ**)_0 = Φ_0 \ Φ_red;
- (Φ**)_1 consists of the composites of the maximal convex chains X1 < x1 < X2 < ... < Xk with x_i ◁ X_i, X_{i+1}.

In modern terms this is a combinatorial 'regularization' operator. The double complement Φ ↦ Φ** is idempotent up to the stated identities, with Φ*** = Φ*. It is the 1-dimensional analogue of passing to a coarsest presentation of a stratified subset of a line, much like int(cl(·)) giving regular open sets, or a closure from a Galois-type complement. The purpose is preparatory: it leads to Ouv^0_M(Φ) and a 'résultat clef' on the next page. This looks like part of a combinatorial theory of tame (modérée) stratified pieces built from flags.
